{
  "id": "nomen",
  "name": "Nouns: people, things, places",
  "level": "A1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "A noun is a name: for a person, an animal, a thing, a place or an idea. In a sentence a noun usually has a, an or the in front of it.",
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "a / an / the", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "noun", "variant": "verb" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">The</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">dog</span> plays with <span class=\"vp-prep\">a</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">ball</span> in <span class=\"vp-prep\">the</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">garden</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "A <strong>noun</strong> is a name for something: a <strong>person</strong> (<em>a teacher</em>), an <strong>animal</strong> (<em>a dog</em>), a <strong>thing</strong> (<em>a table</em>), a <strong>place</strong> (<em>a station</em>) or an <strong>idea</strong> that you cannot touch (<em>fear, happiness</em>). Ask: <strong>who? what?</strong>",
        "tag": "a name"
      },
      {
        "html": "Most nouns start with a <strong>small letter</strong>: <em>My friend lives in a flat.</em> Use a <strong>capital letter</strong> for names of people and places, days, months and languages: <em>Amira, Leeds, Monday, March, English</em>. Not: <em>I live in london.</em>",
        "tag": "capitals"
      },
      {
        "html": "A singular noun that you can count needs <strong>a, an or the</strong>: <em>She is <strong>a</strong> teacher.</em> Not: <em>She is teacher.</em> Learn the rules on the page <a href=\"/articles\">Articles: a, an, the</a>.",
        "tag": "a / an / the"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Countable</strong> nouns can be one or more: <em>a book, two books</em>. <strong>Uncountable</strong> nouns have no plural and no <em>a</em>: <em>water, music, information, advice</em>. We say <em>some information</em>, not <em>an information</em> or <em>informations</em>.",
        "tag": "countable?"
      },
      {
        "html": "We make nouns from verbs and adjectives: <em>teach → teacher, pay → payment, happy → happiness, act → action</em>. Words that end in <strong>-er, -ness, -ment, -tion</strong> are often nouns.",
        "tag": "-er · -ness"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "A quick test: can you put <em>a</em> or <em>the</em> in front of the word? Then it is probably a noun: <em>a house, the idea</em>. Names are nouns too, but they usually have no article: <em>Amira, London, Monday</em>. Next: <a href=\"/articles\">Articles: a, an, the</a> and <a href=\"/plurals\">Plural nouns</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
